<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!--
    Lets the incoming-call notification take over a LOCKED screen instead of
    sitting silently behind the lock. On Android 14+ this is granted at install
    only to apps whose core function is calling or alarms; Localo qualifies
    (in-app voice calls), but it must be declared to Google Play. Without the
    grant nothing breaks — the popup falls back to a heads-up notification that
    still carries Answer and Decline.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT" />

  <application>
    <!--
      ⚠️ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO FirebaseMessagingService HERE.

      One lived here: it outranked expo-notifications' own service and drew the
      CallStyle popup in Kotlin the moment a push landed, so a closed app got a
      real call screen. It never worked on a closed app, and because it consumed
      the message, expo-notifications never got to draw its own — so the phone
      rang with nothing to press, or went silent.

      Three builds settled it. The two that ring correctly (4fff0b9 with no
      service at all, 76655bf where the service bailed on almost everything and
      handed the message back) both end up with expo-notifications drawing the
      notification. The one that stayed silent, e3d4675, is the one where the
      service never handed anything back. Whatever is wrong is inside that
      service, and until a logcat from a phone running it says what, the
      message belongs to Expo: the push carries a title and the incoming_call
      category, so Android renders it with Answer and Decline, on the lock
      screen, without any native code from us.

      CallNotifications is still here and still posts the popup — the call
      alerts check rings the phone through it — so re-enabling this is a
      manifest entry away once we can see why it went quiet.
    -->

    <!--
      Handles the Decline pill without opening the app. Not exported: only our
      own PendingIntent may trigger it, so no other app can dismiss a user's
      incoming call.

      FULLY QUALIFIED on purpose. A relative ".CallActionReceiver" is resolved
      against a package name at manifest-merge time, and this library manifest
      is merged into an app whose namespace is com.localo.app — if it ever
      resolved against that, Decline would throw ClassNotFoundException at the
      moment someone pressed it, with nothing failing at build time to warn us.
    -->
    <receiver
      android:name="expo.modules.callnotification.CallActionReceiver"
      android:exported="false" />
  </application>
</manifest>
